## Commands

Run tooling through `just`, not by calling the tools directly. `just --list` shows every recipe with its description, so use it for anything not listed here.

```bash
just test-fast   # Python tests without coverage: use while iterating
just test        # all tests
just lint        # linters and the type check
just fix         # lint with auto-fix, then format: run before committing
{%- if use_ml %}
just dvc-repro   # reproduce the DVC pipeline
# Any other dvc command has no just recipe: run it directly, e.g. one stage:
uv run --project {{ 'backend' if project_layout == 'product' else '.' }} dvc repro <stage>
{%- endif %}
```
{%- if project_layout == 'product' %}

`just dev` starts the backend and frontend servers and never returns: do not run it yourself; ask the user to.
{%- endif %}

## Development workflow

The skills carry the details; this is only the shape. Start from the right skill and let it run.

1. A loose idea goes to `/grill-with-docs`; a concrete request to `/spec`, which writes `docs/specs/<type>_<name>/` and creates the branch.
2. `implement` follows `/spec` in the same conversation: `tdd` at each seam, then a `code-reviewer` pass against the spec. It never commits.
3. `/update-docs <file>` brings one doc up to date (a README describes the project as it is now; `CHANGELOG.md` records the technical changes and why). `/commit` is the only way commits happen: atomic commits, never a push. A hook blocks any commit until `just test` passes, and another blocks destructive commands.
4. Something broken or slow goes to `/diagnosing-bugs`, not `/spec`. It is manual only: you start it when you want it. The one exception is `implement`, which can send a failure inside its own spec's code to the `debugger` agent.
5. Push and open the PR yourself. Before a release, or after adding an endpoint, authentication, file handling or infrastructure, run `/security-audit`.

